Comment:
Re: Docket USBC-2026-0628 — Decennial Census of the Population of Americans

I respectfully request that the Census Bureau not adopt these proposed regulations without substantially stronger safeguards for demographic data integrity, transparency, accountability, and public challenge.

Accurate population data are fundamental to representative government, civil-rights enforcement, public policy, research, and the equitable allocation of federal resources. The Census Bureau itself recognizes that population estimates are used in federal funding allocations. Removing race and ethnicity questions from the decennial enumeration could make it more difficult to independently measure, validate, and challenge demographic undercounts or disparities affecting particular racial and ethnic populations.

If demographic information remains available only through the ACS, surveys, statistical estimates, or administrative records, the Bureau should publicly establish how those sources will be reconciled with the decennial population count and how citizens, states, researchers, and courts can independently evaluate the resulting data.

I am particularly concerned about any increased reliance on tax returns, W-2s, Social Security-related records, or other administrative identifiers to establish an individual’s residence or population status. Such information should never become an opaque substitute for transparent population measurement. The Bureau should clearly disclose what administrative data are used, the statutory authority for their use, how records are matched, how errors and omissions are identified, how demographic populations are protected from systematic undercounting, and what meaningful procedures exist for correcting inaccurate determinations.

Most importantly, the final rule should preserve sufficient publicly available, statistically valid demographic information for citizens and courts to identify and challenge potential disparities in representation, federal funding, civil-rights enforcement, and government services. Privacy protection and demographic accountability are not mutually exclusive; the federal government should pursue both.

I therefore request that the Census Bureau:

1. Maintain statistically reliable race and ethnicity data sufficient to evaluate population representation and disparities;
2. Publish transparent methodologies for any use of administrative records in population estimation or residence determination;
3. Conduct and publish independent validation and error-rate analyses across racial and ethnic populations;
4. Establish mechanisms for identifying and correcting systematic demographic undercounts;
5. Preserve sufficient data and documentation for independent researchers, states, affected communities, and courts to evaluate and challenge population estimates;
6. Prohibit the use of tax identification, Social Security, tax-reporting, or other administrative data in a manner that could produce undisclosed demographic classifications or materially affect citizens’ rights, representation, or access to federally funded programs without transparent statutory authority, safeguards, and accountability.

A federal statistical system must be capable of protecting individual privacy while simultaneously providing enough reliable information to determine whether every community is being accurately counted and treated equitably. I urge the Census Bureau to strengthen these safeguards before finalizing the proposed rule.

I care about maintaining a fair, accurate, and complete census. Census data and federal datasets based on census data are used to make important decisions affecting our nation’s students, educators, schools, families, and communities.

In FY 2023, approximately $129.8 billion in education spending was guided by census data. Census-driven programs support primary, special, and higher education, as well as education and training programs in communities across the country.

Census data are used for many important purposes, including:

    Allocating federal education funding, including Title I, IDEA, Head Start and other early-childhood programs, and School Nutrition Programs;

    Forecasting changes in student populations and planning for educators, classrooms, and school facilities;

    Identifying the need for resources for emergent multilingual learners, students living in poverty, and students with disabilities;

    Identifying disparities and enforcing federal, state, and local civil rights laws;

    Informing outreach and support programs for first-generation college students;

    Planning and funding community colleges and technical and vocational schools;

    Aligning higher-education programs with regional labor-market needs;

    Determining state higher-education funding formulas;

    Determining student-loan repayment models and income-driven repayment thresholds;

    Planning transportation infrastructure, including roads and public transit; and

    Determining the need for and funding health and social services.

Because census data have such broad consequences, the Census Bureau should take every necessary step to ensure that the decennial census is as complete and accurate as possible.

I ask the Census Bureau to extend the public comment period through December 10, 2026. The proposed changes are consequential and deserve careful review and meaningful public participation. The public needs sufficient time to understand the proposal, assess its potential effects, and provide informed comments.

I oppose the proposed changes to the residence criteria and the proposed regulations concerning demographic questions about race, ethnicity, and sexual orientation. I urge the Census Bureau to withdraw the proposed rule and return with substantial revisions that protect the accuracy and completeness of the census.

In particular, I urge the Census Bureau to:

    Ensure that all people who reside in the United States are included in the apportionment count. The residence criteria should not exclude U.S. residents, including visa holders, refugees, asylum seekers, or other people who live in the United States;

    Revise the proposed enumeration-period guidelines to ensure that people are counted in the correct place on Census Day. The rules should account for people whose residence situations may be complicated, including seasonal workers and recent movers, and should prevent people such as children in foster care and people staying in emergency shelters from being inadvertently excluded;

    Revise the proposed use of tax records so that people whose mailing address differs from their residential address are not incorrectly excluded or assigned to the wrong residence. This is particularly important for people such as service members and people who use post office boxes; and

    Remove the proposed restrictions on demographic questions. Information about race and ethnicity has been collected in the decennial census for generations and is important for understanding population characteristics and enforcing civil rights protections. The Census Bureau should not prohibit the collection of information that may be necessary to understand and address disparities.

Do not prohibit future collection of information about sexual orientation. Although sexual orientation is not currently a question on the decennial census, the Census Bureau should retain the ability to collect demographic information that may be necessary to understand the population. The proposed prohibition should also not interfere with the collection or publication of information about same-sex couples, including data collected through census questions in 2020.

The proposed rule could exclude people from the census count, make population data less accurate, and restrict the collection and publication of information needed to understand our nation's communities. These changes could have significant consequences for representation, federal funding, education, infrastructure, civil rights enforcement, health and social services, and other public programs.

An accurate census depends on counting people where they live and collecting the information necessary to understand our nation's population. I ask the Census Bureau to extend the comment period through December 10, 2026, withdraw the proposed rule, and undertake substantial revisions that protect a complete, accurate, and inclusive census.

I am writing to comment on the proposed rule “Decennial Census of the Population of Americans; Proposed Residence Criteria and Proposed Regulations for Demographic Questions” (Docket No. 260903-0005, Document 2026-18481, published September 10, 2026). 

I am a retired educator and former census taker, and I care about a fair and accurate census because census data and other federal datasets based on the census are used to make key decisions that affect our nation’s students, educators, and schools.  

In FY 2023, $129.8 billion in education spending was guided by census data. Census-driven programs support primary education, special education, and higher education, as well as training and education for community members. Examples of these uses include: 

Federal education funding allocations, including money for Title I, IDEA, Head Start and other early childhood education programs, and School Nutrition Programs; 

Forecasting changes in the size and demographics of student populations and thus the need for educators and school facilities;

Determining the need for resources for emergent multilingual learners, students living in poverty, and students with disabilities;  

Informing outreach and support programs for first-generation college students; 

Planning and funding for community colleges and technical/vocational schools; 

Determining state higher education funding formulas;  

Planning transportation, including roads and public transit; and 

Determining the need for and funding health and social services.  

First, to ensure a fair and accurate census,  the Census Bureau should  extend the comment period until December 10, 2026 as the public needs more time to review and respond. Second, I oppose both the proposed changes to the residence criteria and the proposed regulations regarding demographic questions about race, ethnicity, and sexual orientation. The Census Bureau should  withdraw the proposed rule and return with substantial revisions, including: 

• Eliminate any criteria that would exclude U.S. residents, including visa-holders, refugees, asylum seekers, and others from the apportionment count. The Constitution requires  counting all people living in the United States. 

• Eliminate or substantially revise guidelines for the “enumeration period” so that people (including seasonal workers and recent movers) are counted in the right place on Census Day and so that people (including children in foster care and people in emergency shelters) are not accidentally excluded from the count. 

Revise the tax records language so that those whose mailing address differs from their residential address (servicemembers, those with a post office box) are not excluded.  

• Drop the ban on demographic questions. The U.S. has asked about race in every census since our first in 1790. Information on race and ethnicity is critical for civil rights enforcement, among other uses, and should not be banned. Similarly, sexual orientation, while not currently asked in the census, should not be barred from future censuses. The prohibition, as written, may also restrict the collection or publication of census data on same-sex couples that was asked in 2020. 

 The proposed rule would exclude residents, result in an inaccurate count, and prohibit collection of important data on the characteristics of our population. Such outcomes are unacceptable and would harm all Americans.

Sincerely,
Dr. Ruth Burkett
West Valley City, UT 84120
